We can “trick” any memory allocator by writing our program so that it never keeps more than 4 MB (4096 KB) of live (i.e. not yet freed) allocated memory at any time, but because of the order in which we allocate and free blocks the allocator “extends” the virtual address space by many allocations. One acceptable answer is as follows.

Assume that your machine’s page size is 4 KB. We want the maximum total live malloc’ed memory (i.e. the sum of the sizes of all blocks that have not yet been freed) to be at most 4,096 KB. At the same time, however, we want to ensure that over the run of the program the range of virtual addresses used is at least 5,120 KB (i.e. 1280 pages). (Since 1280×4 KB = 5120 KB.)

The idea is to allocate 4 KB blocks in a loop for 1280 iterations but, once we already have 1024 (4 MB/4 KB) blocks allocated, free the block that was allocated 1024 iterations ago. If the allocator returns blocks at monotonically increasing addresses (or, more generally, if our program is allowed to “inspect” the pointer value and choose its freeing pattern accordingly) then even though at most 1024 blocks are allocated at any one time, the “span” of addresses (i.e. the virtual memory that has been mapped) extends from the first allocated block to the very last allocated block. In the worst‐case, these blocks end up nonoverlapping and in increasing order, so the span will cover 1280 pages (or 5120 KB).

Here is a pseudo–code (or skeleton C program) that describes one such solution:

---------------------------------------------------------
/* Assume: #include <stdlib.h>
   and a page size of 4096 bytes
 */

#define BLOCK_SIZE 4096         /* each block is 4KB */
#define MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS 1024    /* maximum blocks live (4MB total) */
#define TOTAL_BLOCKS 1280       /* total number of allocations (5120 KB over time) */

int main(void) {
    void *blocks[MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS];  // circular buffer to keep track
    int i;

    // First, fill the buffer with the first 1024 blocks.
    for (i = 0; i < MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS; i++) {
        blocks[i] = malloc(BLOCK_SIZE);
        if (!blocks[i]) {
            // Handle allocation failure
            exit(1);
        }
    }

    // Now, for the remaining TOTAL_BLOCKS - MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS iterations:
    // free the oldest block and allocate a new one.
    for (i = MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS; i < TOTAL_BLOCKS; i++) {
        int index = i % MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS;
        free(blocks[index]);
        blocks[index] = malloc(BLOCK_SIZE);
        if (!blocks[index]) {
            // Handle allocation failure
            exit(1);
        }
    }

    // Finally, free all the remaining blocks.
    for (i = 0; i < MAX_LIVE_BLOCKS; i++) {
        free(blocks[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
---------------------------------------------------------

Explanation:

1. The program always has at most 1024 blocks of 4096 bytes (i.e. 4 MB) allocated at a time.
2. However, over its execution it performs 1280 malloc calls that (in the worst‐case “malicious” allocator scenario) return blocks in strictly increasing addresses.
3. The lowest allocated block will come from the very first malloc call, and the highest from the very last one. Thus the overall span of virtual memory will cover 1280×4096 bytes = 5120 KB.
4. Notice that we depend on the pointers returned by malloc (using the circular buffer index) to “force” the allocator into never reusing the freed block’s memory for a later malloc. (In a real-world allocator, freed memory may be reused. However, the problem statement gives us the hint that our program may depend on the pointer values to achieve worst-case behavior.)

Thus, regardless of the particular implementation of malloc (provided the allocator can be “fooled” in this way), our program uses at most 4096 KB of live dynamically allocated memory while “touching” at least 5120 KB of virtual memory over the entire run.

This is one acceptable answer.
